#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085} :activity/id #uuid "69f4a6c0-8376-56ae-a896-808d467721bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048695 :topic/id #uuid "b5a55eb9-858d-5acb-ba88-4503d5523513" :topic/math-academy-id 1001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093360 :tutorial/content "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "87bcd4b8-6965-56b9-8f60-65d778fe217c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1138 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "644bccac-3cde-5911-bd4c-a81e0ad52e7f" :step/math-academy-id 645939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093139}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092091 :question/id #uuid "8a885f78-0028-5b6e-80e6-1215f5d934a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf389905-8964-57b6-85bc-2fd738f05f11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567066}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves With Multiple Intersections"} :step/id #uuid "6fee2e3a-d640-51d8-8bc8-f0525d421f46" :step/math-academy-id 645950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092092 :question/id #uuid "c30d91c2-ee63-5b20-ab39-6d356f711fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-640"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3361ca6a-66d4-515b-b529-c433a85b7a96" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567061}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b48e5386-8b43-5d91-b24a-e05a02d63269" :step/math-academy-id 645946 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092123 :question/id #uuid "dd29a0e8-2912-531e-a44c-62aa6d604273" :question/math-academy-id "e-7361"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01741ca-0ee3-554c-a2c2-788c97d040fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050106}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567007}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Integral Expression For the Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "73d56e4a-2df1-5a1e-a699-7bc22e196b88" :step/math-academy-id 645943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092124 :question/id #uuid "4bc28849-2812-5b53-a2da-36198c06e290" :question/math-academy-id "e-7362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3971880e-a648-58f0-ad82-d87833d62169" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049612}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547036}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Integral Expression For the Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves When the Intersections Are Given"} :step/id #uuid "d481f7d3-47f5-50ee-8b89-5695bdecb5e6" :step/math-academy-id 645940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093138}}] :activity/title "The Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092397}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567122} :activity/id #uuid "42741bb0-86f3-5e45-9a85-6cd852e0c7c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048695 :topic/id #uuid "b5a55eb9-858d-5acb-ba88-4503d5523513" :topic/math-academy-id 1001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc3cf63-f343-587b-a603-cf522db6f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566389} {:answer/id #uuid "63e496ce-cbd3-597e-8724-22c71bcdeac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566554} {:answer/id #uuid "66da9fe1-de4c-5f7b-88cd-f8b50e0cfeaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566558} {:answer/id #uuid "7b56b5a4-2e3f-5ea2-8d9c-9c561a8e3d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566598} {:answer/id #uuid "d019023d-6c4a-5762-8241-f2647647d7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566389} :answer-field/id #uuid "90532304-a931-59ee-84ae-4c5087c14e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00884ded-b155-5156-8a35-6c8781fddcce" :question/math-academy-id "q-83576" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8dd82813c1c47f287771714c08e3ec51dc0affdbc5f7c7df9fedf686bcf02d5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1,$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec2791300fa039fd2a16831b8fcc8d248139fb1e55da2415675f8ea7870e29fd.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "225df473-b06d-5ed2-9cd4-5db150443494" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567139}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149377ee-41da-5426-80f4-7eca214e8353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566403} {:answer/id #uuid "824b7854-95c6-5e28-b075-9f3b0f0b7de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566610} {:answer/id #uuid "879b4088-bbd8-5c61-9ad3-2c11316f7261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566620} {:answer/id #uuid "94ddff8c-3225-52d1-8da7-7cb8fd933e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566655} {:answer/id #uuid "aceeef36-d6cc-56ff-b7e8-3dc517fd523d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566403} :answer-field/id #uuid "94aaec13-9d03-5421-8bca-4274546a15c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff59952d-8c60-571b-985b-2cd62b3bb719" :question/math-academy-id "q-9709" :question/problem "![](images/55/551cca083ab3e8ac624d119b5539574a79636b83c63574880249e52f198bfcb5.png)\n\nFind the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )$ and $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/d0/d0ebb3b99f66e8c1af93f1a15ff4284f5fb9a83fb4d91bad1027e18c8b447939.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))^{2}-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}((\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(0-0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c9037d1-a47f-5cbf-8dc5-bdc6b9714178" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b43da71d-7204-5899-81b0-ccf12dfa9d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12+\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566718} :answer-field/id #uuid "338493ec-0120-5d6b-861d-59ea55d743e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ee30442-2d97-5630-8ccd-d0b392c6ecf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-354137" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b0cf04742382a3658768d688fd4effe9602008c640249b1042939c76c51c4e8.png)\n\nFind the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ and $r=3,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Consider the rightmost petal, oriented along the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/5e/5eee50d0dbcf5c7cb6969416c81eeb8a54eb7ef4df1262e69324718108fc33fa.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}((3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}-(3)^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(9+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )-9)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{12}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+2\\theta +\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{12}{5})+(\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=5{A}_{1}=5(\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5})=12+\\pi .\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45d1172e-cd8b-5dc7-ab8e-bbbe342a4a88"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ddab15d-a213-59cd-9fa7-21cc781db0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566391} {:answer/id #uuid "6e681722-68eb-5e1b-be66-9761b2526ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566570} {:answer/id #uuid "6f68471e-bf27-5ded-b086-2a567f880f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566574} {:answer/id #uuid "84309097-603c-5779-9ab8-18a0c7d5d182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566614} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5c2371-01f6-548a-ab82-c607c80df138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566391} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb14c76-0569-5f28-a8c5-d938b0472df9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9e654085-a432-5396-af7c-98196ef1aed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126408" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea8b9532d6b2b9318f77155a585d46dd18cb168c455318bd5abe72c4a11a4500.png)\n\nFind the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ and $r=1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Consider the mostright petal along $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/a1/a1129012105492fb870dbbc5960008b984ea98a9f61c267b92c1b0d695ee838c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )))^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})+(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12})+\\frac{1}{12}(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=3{A}_{1}=3(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{12})=2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9d7b024c-48f6-5526-b679-f115e653418c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567142}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e78b876-2391-5925-9f6b-e1686b55096a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566489} :answer-field/id #uuid "5961090d-3a51-57b9-a935-949cdac567c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8ea911ef-18d1-54db-a83b-3b4bb372d13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353117" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eec15c94d63cab750a0dc15525d800452a1a1dbdd3e948dff8ef8d6cf0e2abc7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/9b/9bcc7175d07a6d9bb4c3fa202f3ad711effa962e3c9a798b3869f9601d4214c3.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}((\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b3d5486d-b3ba-54b8-b78c-0e757a583b54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567126}}] :activity/title "The Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566353}]}
